                <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Cinder Block Sealing Mistakes
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    Concrete block is far more porous than poured concrete, and the mortar joints between the blocks are more porous still. Sealing it well means respecting both.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling It On Like Paint</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Block sealers work by filling pores, and a smooth rolled film skips over exactly the pinholes water travels through. Work the first coat in hard with a stiff masonry brush, paying particular attention to the mortar joints, and use the roller only for the second coat.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Efflorescence</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        The chalky white bloom on block is mineral salt carried out by evaporating water, and no coating bonds to it. It has to be wire-brushed off and washed down with a masonry cleaner first. Sealing over it produces a coating that lifts away in patches, taking the powder with it.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Sealer to Stop Hydrostatic Pressure</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Masonry waterproofing coatings resist damp and vapour well and standing water poorly. Where groundwater is pressing against the outside of the wall, the coating will blister off from behind regardless of how carefully it went on. That is a drainage problem, and it has to be solved outside the wall.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Under-Applying to Save Product</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Coverage rates on block are far lower than on smooth concrete — often half — because the pores swallow material. Stretching a pail to hit the number on the label leaves a thin coat that fails early. Buy for the actual surface and put down the full recommended thickness across two coats.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Closing -->
                <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
                  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    The best <strong class="text-white">cinder block sealer</strong> depends on which side of the wall you are on and what you need it to do. Cementitious waterproofers give the most resistance to damp on interior basement walls, penetrating silanes and siloxanes protect exterior block without changing its look, and acrylic coatings are the choice where appearance matters as much as protection.
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                    Whatever you choose, preparation carries the result: brush off efflorescence, wash the surface, work the first coat into the pores and joints with a stiff brush, and apply the full recommended thickness. And if water is under pressure from outside, fix the drainage first — no coating wins that fight from the inside.
                  </p>
                </div>

